{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-5ecfae64-530c-4c11-97d4-843a97132177","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c05f000a33531b9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-tpsk22jc/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E005.md","content":"# External OIDC authentication enablement in MaaS\n\n## Description\n\nReplaces OpenShift-specific identity infrastructure (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift\nAPI server) with external OIDC validation for MaaS on xKS. Customers provide their own\nOIDC-compatible identity provider (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, or\nother standards-compliant providers). The API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*` bearer tokens\nvalidated via the maas-api callback endpoint) is OIDC-agnostic and remains unchanged — its\ncontinued function on xKS is validated here.\n\nThe approach is gated on the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline changeability assessment: the strategy expects\nbasic OIDC integration to be a configuration change (the Tenant CRD already supports\n`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`), but if the baseline requires code changes the\napproach is rewritten accordingly.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Configure the MaaS-generated AuthPolicy resources to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source\n  (customer OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint) instead of KubernetesTokenReview.\n- Plumb OIDC issuer configuration through the Helm chart values and Tenant CRD fields.\n- Adapt internal maas-api authentication paths that rely on Kubernetes TokenReview /\n  SubjectAccessReview (OpenShift identity mapping) for the xKS deployment model.\n- Preserve the API key validation callback path (`/internal/v1/api-keys/validate` via Authorino\n  HTTP callback) and confirm it is unaffected by the OIDC change.\n- Validate against at least one standards-compliant OIDC provider, with the design supporting\n  varied providers (Entra ID, Cognito, Google Identity Platform).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance on xKS with OIDC configured, a user authenticating with a valid\n  OIDC JWT token is authenticated; invalid or expired tokens are rejected.\n- API key authentication (the `sk-oai-*` flow) functions on xKS without OIDC dependency:\n  inference requests using valid API keys succeed.\n- Failure mode: when the OIDC provider becomes unavailable, inference requests using valid API\n  keys continue to succeed, while OIDC-authenticated requests fail with HTTP 503 and a\n  descriptive error message.\n- Backwards compatibility: no changes to RHOAI MaaS GA behavior — xKS changes are additive or\n  configuration-driven.\n- The OIDC identity flow works with at least one standards-compliant provider and the\n  configuration surface supports provider-specific issuer/JWKS values.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n- [P0] API key authentication (`sk-oai-*` flow) functional on xKS.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64348,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90b1b6f56466ed62","agent_id":"a8a2b528182aa5e23","session_id":"d2b1376c-e550-4ef4-93a1-44e607f6014e","uuid":"0f0747d5-17ce-402c-bf5c-14111446cdb7","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:00:12.900Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8003"}
