{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8d53dda6-ec24-496e-8ca4-22ac55a58b27","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-be07ee9c17d272b4","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8003-tpsk22jc/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E008.md","content":"# Deploy and validate MaaS on CKS and AKS\n\n## Description\n\nDeploys MaaS via the Helm charts on CKS first (the priority platform, with Istio GatewayClass and\nmTLS configuration for CoreWeave's service mesh), then on AKS, and validates core MaaS\nfunctionality: subscription/tier management, token rate limiting, API key authentication, and\nexternal OIDC authentication. Documents what works, what breaks, and the engineering delta —\nfeeding the known-limitations documentation. The Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) underlay is\ndeployed using the mechanism confirmed by the RHCL xKS support validation, with the strategy's\nfallback ladder (standalone Authorino + Limitador via Helm; direct Envoy ext_authz contingency)\navailable if official RHCL support is not committed.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Deploy MaaS on CKS via Helm with OIDC configuration and the Istio GatewayClass (including TLS\n  and mTLS configuration for Istio control-plane integration).\n- Deploy MaaS on AKS via Helm (GatewayClass per platform: `azure-application-gateway` or\n  `envoy-gateway`).\n- Validate core functionality on both platforms: subscription management, token rate limiting,\n  API key lifecycle, OIDC authentication, tier mapping with OIDC group claims.\n- Validate failure modes: OIDC provider unavailability, Limitador pod termination, cert-manager\n  certificate rotation, PostgreSQL unavailability.\n- Document what works vs. what breaks and the engineering delta (code changes, configuration\n  changes, Helm chart packaging gaps).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a CKS cluster with RHCL/Kuadrant and Gateway API (Istio) installed, when MaaS is deployed\n  via Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then the maas-api and maas-controller pods are running\n  and healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given an AKS cluster with RHCL/Kuadrant and Gateway API installed, when MaaS is deployed via\n  Helm chart with OIDC configuration, then the maas-api and maas-controller pods are running and\n  healthy, measured by pod readiness checks passing.\n- Given a deployed MaaS instance on CKS with OIDC configured, when a user authenticates with a\n  valid OIDC JWT token and creates an API key, then the API key is persisted in PostgreSQL and\n  can be used for model inference, measured by a successful inference request using the generated\n  API key.\n- Given a MaaSSubscription with token rate limits configured, when a user exceeds the rate limit,\n  then Limitador returns HTTP 429, measured by monitoring response status codes.\n- Failure mode — OIDC provider unavailable: API key requests continue to succeed; OIDC requests\n  fail with HTTP 503 and a descriptive error.\n- Failure mode — Limitador pod terminated: inference requests denied with HTTP 503, measured by\n  response status and Envoy logs showing upstream connection failure.\n- Failure mode — cert-manager TLS rotation: active inference requests continue without\n  interruption; new requests succeed with the new certificate.\n- Failure mode — PostgreSQL unavailable: API key creation fails with HTTP 503 and existing API\n  key validation fails on cache miss, while OIDC token-based authentication continues to succeed.\n- What works, what breaks, and the engineering delta are documented for both platforms.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] MaaS successfully deployed on CKS.\n- [P0] MaaS successfully deployed on AKS.\n- [P0] Token rate limiting functional on xKS.\n- [P0] External OIDC authentication validated for user identity.\n- [P0] API key authentication (`sk-oai-*` flow) functional on xKS.\n- [P1] Tier/subscription management functional with 