{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ade2f92f7a491373","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tepic_count: 11\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8003 — RHAII MaaS on xKS\n11\t\n12\tTriage: **not triaged** — the strategy is M-sized (3-5 sprints), spans multiple components\n13\t(models-as-a-service, Kuadrant, Gateway API, observability) and three teams (MaaS, xKS Platform,\n14\tRHCL / OpenShift), with HLRs at P0/P1/P2. Both Step 0 escape conditions for below-threshold are\n15\tirrelevant at this size; no check fired, so full decomposition (Steps 1-8) was executed.\n16\t\n17\tStaff Engineer Input: the section contains only template placeholder comments (no content), so\n18\tthere is nothing to parse or override. See Health Warnings #1 — the strategy body repeatedly\n19\tcites \"per Staff Engineer Input\" although the section is empty.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List (main plan)\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n25\t| E001 | Confirm RHCL xKS support and validate Kuadrant on CKS | Investigation | RHCL / OpenShift | P0 |\n26\t| E002 | Assess MaaS 3.4 GA baseline changeability for xKS | Investigation | MaaS | P0 |\n27\t| E003 | Validate CKS observability infrastructure accessibility | Investigation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n28\t| E004 | Helm chart packaging for MaaS components on xKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n29\t| E005 | External OIDC authentication enablement in MaaS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n30\t| E007 | Observability instrumentation in maas-api (metrics and structured logging) | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n31\t| E008 | Deploy and validate MaaS on CKS and AKS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n32\t| E009 | CKS observability integration: Perses dashboards, Prometheus, Loki | Implementation | xKS Platform | P0 |\n33\t| E010 | Structured logging for Authorino and Limitador on xKS | Implementation | RHCL / OpenShift | P1 |\n34\t| E011 | Distributed tracing through the MaaS request path | Implementation | MaaS | P2 |\n35\t| E012 | Document known limitations and user identity flow for MaaS on xKS | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n36\t\n37\tEpic count: 11 main-plan epics. Slot E006 is occupied by conditional branch epics (below), which\n38\tare plan documents attached to the gating Investigation, not standalone Jira issues.\n39\t\n40\t### Conditional branch epics (gated by E002 outcome)\n41\t\n42\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n43\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n44\t| BRANCH-A-E006 | Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping in MaaS | Implementation | MaaS | P0 |\n45\t| BRANCH-B-E006 | Document required OIDC group claim format for xKS identity providers | Implementation (docs-authoring) | MaaS | P0 |\n46\t\n47\tE002 has two bounded outcomes: (A) the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline is extensible — the Tenant CRD can\n48\tcarry a `groupClaimPath` field and the code change proceeds; (B) the Tenant CRD is frozen — the\n49\tconfigurable mapping is not implementable and the strategy's stated fallback applies (document\n50\tthe required claim format, push IdP configuration burden to customers). Outcome A and B produce\n51\tstructurally different epics (code vs. documentation), so they are modeled as conditional\n52\tbranches rather than a single gated epic.\n53\t\n54\t## Dependency DAG\n55\t\n56\t```mermaid\n57\tgraph TD\n58\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: RHCL xKS support / Kuadrant on CKS\"] --> E008[\"E008 Deploy & validate CKS+AKS\"]\n59\t    E001 --> E010[\"E010 Authorino/Limitador structured logging\"]\n60\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: 3.4 baseline changeability\"] --> E005[\"E005 External OIDC authentication\"]\n61\t    E002 --> E007[\"E007 maas-api observability instrumentation\"]\n62\t    E002 --> E008\n63\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: CKS observability infra\"] --> E009[\"E009 CKS observability integration\"]\n64\t    E003 --> E010\n65\t    E004[\"E004 MaaS Helm chart packaging\"] --> E008\n66\t    E005 --> E008\n67\t    E007 --> E009\n68\t    E008 --> E009\n69\t    E008 --> E011[\"E011 Distributed tracing\"]\n70\t    E004 --> E012[\"E012 Known limitations & identity flow docs\"]\n71\t    E005 --> E012\n72\t    E007 --> E012\n73\t    E008 --> E012\n74\t    E009 --> E012\n75\t    E010 --> E012\n76\t    E011 --> E012\n77\t    E002 -.-> E006A[\"BRANCH-A-E006 groupClaimPath implementation\"]\n78\t    E002 -.-> E006B[\"BRANCH-B-E006 claim format documentation\"]\n79\t```\n80\t\n81\tDashed edges select conditional branches; branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n82\t\n83\t## DAG Justification\n84\t\n85\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n86\t| --- | --- | --- |\n87\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether the existing AuthPolicy generation can leverage Authorino external OIDC without code changes (vs. needing changes) rewrites the authentication epic's approach. Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n88\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 3 | If maas-api is frozen in 3.4 GA, instrumentation must ship as a post-3.4 patch or be deferred (strategy assumption). Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n89\t| E002 → E008 | Rule 3 + branch rule 2 | Deployment validation covers tier mapping, which depends on the group-claim resolution. It cannot depend on branch epics, so it depends on the Investigation itself. |\n90\t| E002 ⇢ BRANCH-A/B-E006 | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes, 2) | The Tenant-CRD-frozen conditional is stated explicitly in the strategy with a defined fallback; outcomes produce structurally different epics → conditional branches. |\n91\t| E001 → E008 | Rules 3, 16, 21 | The Kuadrant deployment mechanism (RHCL official Helm vs. standalone Authorino+Limitador vs. direct Envoy ext_authz) is selected by E001. Per Rule 21 the viable fallback ladder is noted as ACs/fallback, not duplicated as epics. Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n92\t| E001 → E010 | Rule 3 | Structured logging for Authorino/Limitador is configured on the deployed Kuadrant stack, whose form depends on E001's outcome. Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n93\t| E003 → E009 | Rules 3, 22 | CKS Prometheus/Loki is infrastructure assumed available but flagged \"Needs validation\". If inaccessible, the integration epic is rewritten to provision dedicated observability. Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n94\t| E003 → E010 | Rule 3 | Loki aggregation is the destination for Authorino/Limitador structured logs; accessibility is validated by E003. |\n95\t| E004 → E008 | Rule 13 | The Helm charts are the build-against artifact the deployment validation consumes. |\n96\t| E005 → E008 | Rules 12/13 | Functional validation of OIDC authentication and API keys requires the adapted authentication stack. |\n97\t| E007 → E009 | Rule 13 | Perses dashboards and Prometheus integration consume the metrics maas-api emits. |\n98\t| E008 → E009 | Rule 13 | Dashboard/logging validation runs against a deployed MaaS instance on CKS. |\n99\t| E008 → E011 | Rule 13 | Tracing spans the deployed request path (gateway → Authorino → Limitador → payload-processing → backend). |\n100\t| E004/E005/E007/E008/E009/E010/E011 → E012 | Rule 11 | `docs-authoring` is blocked by all Implementation epics — docs describe what was built. Per the docs priority exception these edges do not push priority upstream; E012's P0 derives from strategy priority `Blocker` (Critical-equivalent). Branch epics are not main-plan nodes and are legitimately absent from E012's dependencies; the selected branch completes before the docs epic starts (it is downstream of E002 and of every implementation path). |\n101\t\n102\tParallel by design (no edge, deliberately): E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 all start immediately\n103\t(independent inputs). E004 ∥ E005 ∥ E007 are same-repo different-area work (Rule 15 — merge\n104\tconflicts are coordination risk, not dependency). No konflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding chain\n105\texists (component is in the platform), so Rules 5-10 ordering constraints and the Rule 25\n106\tbuild-pipeline AC do not apply; the pending Helm build-pipeline process gate is captured as an\n107\tacceptance criterion on E004.\n108\t\n109\t## Investigation Decisions\n110\t\n111\tUnknowns swept from the open-questions table, risks table, assumptions table, and dependencies\n112\ttable; each evaluated against \"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\"\n113\t\n114\t| Unknown (source) | Decision | Handling |\n115\t| --- | --- | --- |\n116\t| RHCL official xKS support + Kuadrant installability on CKS (dependency \"Unknown\"; assumptions \"Needs validation (critical)\"; open questions; risk table) | Changes what the deployment epic does | **Investigation E001**, gating E008/E010 with a rewrite gate and fallback ladder as ACs (Rule 21) |\n117\t| Can existing MaaS AuthPolicy leverage external OIDC; is Tenant CRD extensible with `groupClaimPath`; can maas-api be instrumented without 3.4 GA code changes (open questions; assumptions \"Needs validation\") | Changes epic content/existence (group-claim work becomes documentation if CRD frozen) | **Investigation E002** (one experiment — inspect the 3.4 GA baseline and its freeze constraints — answers all three; combined per Step 3); conditional branches BRANCH-A/B-E006 |\n118\t| CKS Prometheus/Loki accessibility (assumptions \"Needs validation\") | Changes the observability integration approach | **Investigation E003**, gating E009/E010 |\n119\t| MaaS 3.4 GA stable build timing (dependency table; open question owned by MaaS PM) | Does not change which epics exist — work proceeds against RC then stable build; failure = fallback demo | Acceptance criterion / timeline risk on E002, E004, E008; health warning #5 |\n120\t| Shared vs. dedicated PostgreSQL (open question, xKS Platform) | Config decision resolved at implementation time; chart makes it configurable | AC on E004 |\n121\t| OIDC validation caching strategy during IdP outages (open question) | The failure-mode ACs already define required behavior; caching is an implementation decision | ACs on E005/E008 |\n122\t| What authentication approach for xKS (open question) | Resolved by the strategy itself: external OIDC with customer-provided IdP | Reflected in E005 scope |\n123\t\n124\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n125\t\n126\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n127\t| --- | --- | --- |\n128\t| MaaS successfully deployed on CKS | P0 | E008 |\n129\t| MaaS successfully deployed on AKS | P0 | E008 |\n130\t| Token rate limiting functional on xKS | P0 | E001 (mechanism), E008 (validation) |\n131\t| External OIDC authentication validated | P0 | E005, E008 |\n132\t| API key authentication (sk-oai-* flow) functional on xKS | P0 | E005 (flow preserved), E008 (validation) |\n133\t| Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping | P0 | BRANCH-A-E006 / BRANCH-B-E006 |\n134\t| RHCL official xKS support status confirmed, mechanism validated | P0 | E001 |\n135\t| Observability metrics functional | P0 | E007 (instrumentation), E009 (dashboards/Prometheus) |\n136\t| Tier/subscription management functional with OIDC group claims | P1 | BRANCH-A-E006 / BRANCH-B-E006, E008 (validation) |\n137\t| Observability logging functional | P1 | E007 (maas-api logs), E010 (Authorino/Limitador logs), E009 (Loki aggregation) |\n138\t| Known limitations documented | P1 | E012 |\n139\t| User identity flow documented for xKS | P2 | E012 |\n140\t| Distributed tracing functional | P2 | E011 |\n141\t\n142\tAll 13 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority inheritance: E001/E002/E003 are P0\n143\tbecause they block P0 work. The P1 logging HLR is bundled into E007 (P0) because maas-api metrics\n144\tand structured logging share the same instrumentation surface — not distinct and deferrable per\n145\tStep 4. The P2 tracing HLR is split into E011: it is a distinct, deferrable feature with\n146\tindependent user value. E010 is P1 (its HLR is P1; it blocks no P0 work). E012 is P0 via the\n147\tdocs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority `Blocker` → Critical-equivalent → P0), not\n148\tvia inheritance.\n149\t\n150\t## Component Mappings (canonical names from .context/rhai-components.txt)\n151\t\n152\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Notes |\n153\t| --- | --- | --- |\n154\t| models-as-a-service (maas-api, maas-controller, payload-processing) | Model as a Service | Direct match; architecture context `models-as-a-service.md` exists |\n155\t| Kuadrant (Authorino, Limitador) | Model as a Service | No canonical Kuadrant component exists; Kuadrant is MaaS's auth/rate-limit enforcement layer, documented inside `models-as-a-service.md` — closest parent-level component |\n156\t| Gateway API | (no separate epic) | The change (xKS-native GatewayClass instead of `openshift-default`, Istio for CKS) is Helm chart configuration inside E004; xKS Platform collaborates via the RHAII installation-integration AC |\n157\t| Observability (Prometheus, Perses, Loki) | Monitoring | Architecture context `odh-observability.md` exists |\n158\t| Documentation deliverables (known limitations, identity flow, claim format) | Documentation | docs-authoring work product |\n159\t\n160\t## Health Warnings\n161\t\n162\t1. **Empty Staff Engineer Input with in-body citations.** The Staff Engineer Input section\n163\t   contains only template placeholder comments, yet the strategy body cites \"per Staff Engineer\n164\t   Input\" repeatedly (CKS as priority platform, observability as core value proposition, RHCL\n165\t   criticality). The decomposition follows the strategy body as written; a human should confirm\n166\t   these attributions, since Step 1.5 precedence could not be applied.\n167\t2. **Acceptance-criterion contradiction on platform coverage.** The RFE acceptance criteria say\n168\t   \"at least on xKS platform (AKS AND CKS)\" while the Proposed Solution says \"at least one xKS\n169\t   platform (AKS or CKS)\". The HLR list