tiple teams.\nNo work-product type mixing — code and content authoring are in separate epics. No\nscope duplication: E007/E010/E009 split observability along clean lines (maas-api\ninstrumentation / Kuadrant logging config / CKS integration).\n\nMinor: E008 is slightly oversized — it bundles CKS deployment (Istio, mTLS, OIDC), AKS\ndeployment, full functional validation (subscriptions, rate limiting, API keys, OIDC,\ntier mapping), four failure-mode validations, and engineering-delta documentation for\nboth platforms. Completable in a single focused sprint at Dev Preview depth, but the\nlargest epic in the set (level-1 per the rubric).\n\nMinor: in outcome B, E012's identity-flow documentation (\"group-claim-based tier\nmapping\") overlaps BRANCH-B's claim-format documentation; the split of that content\nbetween the two docs epics is not drawn explicitly.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n\nAll three Investigations (E001, E002, E003) genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes\ndownstream structure, and each has downstream dependents. All Implementation epics\nproduce concrete artifacts. Every epic with non-null `gated_by` has both `action` and\n`fallback_approach` populated, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct\n`dependencies`. Branch files have correct `branch`/`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`\n(remove + fallback) metadata.\n\nLevel-1 gating metadata gap: E008 lists two Investigations in `dependencies` (E001,\nE002) but its single `gated_by` covers only E001; likewise E010 lists E001 and E003 but\ngates only on E001. For E008 the E002 dependency is justified as a sequencing stand-in\nfor the un-referenceable branch epic, and for E010 the E003 dependency is a\ndestination-availability check — arguable rather than wrong given `gated_by` is\nsingle-valued, but the metadata does not express that the second Investigation's\noutcome also bears on these epics.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal rubric types are correct: Investigations (E001-E003) carry `investigation_signals`\nwith `ai_signals: null`; all Implementation epics (including branches and docs-authoring)\ncarry `ai_signals` with `investigation_signals: null`. All 14 ai-signals files exist and\ntheir rationale tables match the frontmatter values exactly. Signal values are consistent\nwith rubric conditions and strategy content: E001's `source_accessibility: 0` /\n`human_judgment_required: -2` correctly reflect that the gating answer is a pending\norganizational decision; E005/E007's `open_questions: -1` correctly reflect their gating\non unresolved baseline-changeability questions while E008's `open_questions: 0` is\ncorrect (its unknowns are resolved by dependencies); E009's `repo_access: 0` fits\ncluster-targeted work; BRANCH-A/B's `open_questions: 0` is correct given the branch\npremise resolves the gate. No arithmetic or threshold checking performed (computed by\npipeline).\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable, strategy-derived acceptance criteria, mostly in given/when/then\nform. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E005 (a replacement of the\nidentity mechanism) carries the backwards-compatibility AC; both docs-authoring epics\n(E012, BRANCH-B) carry technical-review ACs; E004 carries the Helm build-pipeline and\nlicense-validation ACs standing in for the (N/A) konflux chain. No epic body references\nsibling epics by draft ID — gating and dependency relationships are described in prose,\nnot by E-number.\n\nMinor: E008's scope includes \"tier mapping with OIDC group claims\" and subscription\nmanagement validation, but its AC list has no corresponding criterion — tier-mapping\nenforcement appears only in BRANCH-A's ACs, leaving E008's validation portion of the\nP1 tier/subscription HLR without its own testable check.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nThe combined epic set accounts for all strategy scope: user management strategy (E005 +\nbranches), Helm packaging (E004), Kuadrant/RHCL mechanism (E001), CKS+AKS deployment\nvalidation (E008), all three observability tiers (E007/E009/E010/E011), known\nlimitations and identity flow documentation (E012), and the strategy's failure-mode\nacceptance criteria (E005/E008). All happy-path criteria from the strategy appear in\nepic ACs. Open questions and risks are swept and dispositioned in the Investigation\nDecisions table (built availability timing, PostgreSQL sharing, OIDC caching all become\nACs/risks rather than being dropped). Conditional branches cover both bounded outcomes\nof E002 (extensible vs. frozen Tenant CRD). Strategy-level context — the empty Staff\nEngineer Input contradiction, the AKS \"AND vs OR\" acceptance-criterion contradiction,\nthe Blocker priority mapping — is carried forward as health warnings rather than\nsilently dropped. The PostgreSQL LRU cache risk is explicitly dispositioned (optional\nmitigation, health warning #7). Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\" (not\n\"Yes\") and a docs-authoring epic exists anyway, so Rule 11 is satisfied.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":41509,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a28edd69652dfb8f","agent_id":"aa81afe1864591cbd","session_id":"d2b1376c-e550-4ef4-93a1-44e607f6014e","uuid":"7b2b947e-51dc-430a-b41a-5d8284eda98b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:10:12.018Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8003 decomposition"}
