{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c227e2312fd0e31","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8003\n3\tscore: 10\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing dependency edge between E010 and E008: E010 (Authorino/Limitador\n10\t    structured logging) depends only on E001 and E003, but its acceptance criteria\n11\t    presuppose a deployed MaaS instance on CKS (E008''s artifact) and its scope configures\n12\t    the Kuadrant underlay that E008 deploys. The analogous E009 has an E008 edge for\n13\t    exactly this rationale. Add the edge (or restructure E010 so its validation does\n14\t    not consume E008''s deployment).'\n15\t- severity: minor\n16\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n17\t  description: Summary claims the selected E006 branch completes before E012 starts,\n18\t    but nothing in the DAG enforces it — E012 has no dependency path through the selected\n19\t    branch, so the branch could still be open when E012 authors group-claim documentation.\n20\t- severity: minor\n21\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n22\t  description: 'E008 is slightly oversized: bundles CKS and AKS deployment, full functional\n23\t    validation, four failure-mode validations, and engineering-delta documentation;\n24\t    consider splitting platform deployment validation from failure-mode/delta documentation.'\n25\t- severity: minor\n26\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n27\t  description: In outcome B, E012's identity-flow documentation (group-claim-based\n28\t    tier mapping) overlaps BRANCH-B's claim-format documentation; draw the content\n29\t    split between the two docs epics explicitly.\n30\t- severity: minor\n31\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n32\t  description: E008 (dependencies E001+E002) and E010 (dependencies E001+E003) each\n33\t    list a second Investigation dependency not covered by their single gated_by value;\n34\t    the gating metadata does not express that the second Investigation outcome (E002\n35\t    branch resolution, E003 Loki accessibility) also bears on these epics.\n36\t- severity: minor\n37\t  criterion: Acceptance Criteria Quality\n38\t  description: E008 scope includes tier mapping with OIDC group claims and subscription\n39\t    management validation, but its AC list has no corresponding criterion; add a testable\n40\t    tier-mapping/subscription-validation AC to E008.\n41\terror: null\n42\t---\n43\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8003\n44\t\n45\t## Review Summary\n46\t\n47\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n48\tRecommendation: revise\n49\t\n50\tAuto-fail applied: Criterion 2 (DAG Coherence) scores 0 due to a critical missing\n51\tdependency edge (E010 ↔ E008), so the decomposition fails regardless of the total\n52\tscore of 10.\n53\t\n54\t## Criterion Details\n55\t\n56\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n57\t\n58\tAll 13 HLRs from the strategy map to at least one epic via the traceability matrix,\n59\tincluding the P0 \"Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping\" HLR, which is correctly\n60\tmapped to the conditional branches (BRANCH-A/B-E006) since the strategy itself makes\n61\tthat HLR's delivery form conditional on the Tenant CRD freeze outcome. P2 HLRs (identity\n62\tflow documentation, distributed tracing) are covered by E012 and E011 respectively, not\n63\tdeferred. Priority inheritance holds: E001/E002/E003 are P0 Investigations blocking P0\n64\twork; E010 is P1 and blocks only the docs-authoring epic E012 (Rule 11 edges do not\n65\ttrigger priority inheritance); E012's P0 derives correctly from the docs-authoring\n66\tpriority exception (strategy priority Blocker treated as Critical-equivalent, flagged\n67\tas health warning #3). The P1 logging HLR bundled into E007 (P0) is justified — metrics\n68\tand structured logging in maas-api share one instrumentation surface and are not\n69\tindependently deferrable. The P2 tracing HLR is correctly split into deferrable E011.\n70\t\n71\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n72\t\n73\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check: every edge in the summary DAG diagram has a matching\n74\t`dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and vice versa; arrows run\n75\tdependency → dependent per convention. Branch files both set `branch` and `gated_by`;\n76\tno main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies` (E008 correctly depends on\n77\tthe Investigation E002 rather than a branch). No konflux/repo-onboarding chain exists,\n78\tso rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are N/A. No circular dependencies.\n79\t\n80\tCritical issue — missing edge between E010 and E008: E010 (Authorino/Limitador\n81\tstructured logging) lists only E001 and E003 as dependencies, yet its own acceptance\n82\tcriteria presuppose \"a deployed MaaS instance on CKS\" — the artifact E008 produces —\n83\tand its scope configures the Kuadrant underlay that E008 deploys (\"The Kuadrant\n84\t(Authorino + Limitador) underlay is deployed using the mechanism confirmed by...\").\n85\tThe parallel epic E009 received the E008 edge for exactly this rationale (\"Dashboard/\n86\tlogging validation runs against a deployed MaaS instance on CKS\"), making the omission\n87\ton E010 an internal inconsistency. As scheduled, E010 could start and its ACs could be\n88\t\"verified\" before any MaaS deployment exists. This is a data/artifact dependency\n89\tdiscoverable from epic content and not captured in the DAG — critical, criterion\n90\tscores 0.\n91\t\n92\tMinor: critical path length is 5 against the M-standard heuristic of 3-4 (no new\n93\tcomponent); the excess comes from the mandatory Rule 11 docs chain (E012), so this is\n94\tan observation, not a deduction (criterion already 0).\n95\t\n96\tMinor: the summary asserts \"the selected branch completes before the docs epic starts\"\n97\tbut nothing in the DAG enforces it — E012 has no dependency path through the selected\n98\tE006 branch, so BRANCH-A could in principle still be open when E012 begins authoring\n99\tthe group-claim portion of the identity-flow doc.\n100\t\n101\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (1/2)\n102\t\n103\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: MaaS epics (E002, E004, E005, E007, E008,\n104\tE011), xKS Platform epics (E003, E009 on Monitoring), RHCL/OpenShift epics (E001, E010),\n105\tdocs-authoring epics (E012, BRANCH-B on Documentation). No epic bundles multiple teams.\n106\tNo work-product type mixing — code and content authoring are in separate epics. No\n107\tscope duplication: E007/E010/E009 split observability along clean lines (maas-api\n108\tinstrumentation / Kuadrant logging config / CKS integration).\n109\t\n110\tMinor: E008 is slightly oversized — it bundles CKS deployment (Istio, mTLS, OIDC), AKS\n111\tdeployment, full functional validation (subscriptions, rate limiting, API keys, OIDC,\n112\ttier mapping), four failure-mode validations, and engineering-delta documentation for\n113\tboth platforms. Completable in a single focused sprint at Dev Preview depth, but the\n114\tlargest epic in the set (level-1 per the rubric).\n115\t\n116\tMinor: in outcome B, E012's identity-flow documentation (\"group-claim-based tier\n117\tmapping\") overlaps BRANCH-B's claim-format documentation; the split of that content\n118\tbetween the two docs epics is not drawn explicitly.\n119\t\n120\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n121\t\n122\tAll three Investigations (E001, E002, E003) genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes\n123\tdownstream structure, and each has downstream dependents. All Implementation epics\n124\tproduce concrete artifacts. Every epic with non-null `gated_by` has both `action` and\n125\t`fallback_approach` populated, and every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct\n126\t`dependencies`. Branch files have correct `branch`/`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact`\n127\t(remove + fallback) metadata.\n128\t\n129\tLevel-1 gating metadata gap: E008 lists two Investigations in `dependencies` (E001,\n130\tE002) but its single `gated_by`