{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e4e943377640417","type":"tool_result","content":"==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E001.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E001\ntitle: Confirm RHCL xKS support and validate Kuadrant on CKS\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Model as a Service\nteam: RHCL / OpenShift\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P0\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 0\n  local_runnability: 0\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n  human_judgment_required: -2\nimplementation_type: null\ndependencies: []\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Low\nai_implementability_score: -3\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Confirm RHCL xKS support and validate Kuadrant on CKS\n\n## Description\n\nResolves the strategy's strategic dependency on RHCL (Red Hat Common Libraries) for MaaS on xKS.\nConfirms whether the OpenShift RHCL team commits to official xKS support and Helm-based\ndistribution for Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador), and hands-on validates that Kuadrant installs\nand functions on CKS with the Istio GatewayClass. The deliverable is a documented decision on the\nMaaS xKS deployment mechanism for authentication and rate limiting, selected from the strategy's\npriority ladder: (1) official RHCL Helm charts, (2) standalone Authorino + Limitador via Helm,\n(3) direct Envoy ext_authz integration. If RHCL support is not committed, the escalation to\nproduct leadership is recorded and the fallback path is activated.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Engage the OpenShift RHCL team to obtain a commitment (or explicit non-commitment) and timeline\n  for official xKS support and official Kuadrant Helm charts.\n- Validate a Kuadrant (Authorino + Limitador) installation on a CKS cluster with the Istio\n  GatewayClass, per the strategy's Sprint 1 validation task.\n- Exercise the Authorino OIDC identity source and Limitador token rate limiting in that\n  installation to confirm they function on non-OpenShift Kubernetes.\n- Evaluate the fallback ladder (standalone Helm deployment; direct Envoy ext_authz contingency)\n  and document which rung applies.\n- If RHCL support is not committed: record the escalation to product leadership per the strategy.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- RHCL official xKS support status is documented (committed or not committed, with timeline).\n- Kuadrant installability on CKS with Istio is validated with pass/fail evidence (pods running,\n  AuthPolicy enforceable, TokenRateLimitPolicy enforceable).\n- The deployment mechanism for MaaS xKS is selected from the priority ladder with rationale.\n- If not committed: escalation to product leadership is recorded and the selected fallback\n  (standalone Authorino + Limitador, or direct Envoy ext_authz) is documented with its\n  implications for long-term support.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E002.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ntitle: Assess MaaS 3.4 GA baseline changeability for xKS\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Model as a Service\nteam: MaaS\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P0\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 1\n  local_runnability: 0\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n  human_judgment_required: -1\nimplementation_type: null\ndependencies: []\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Medium\nai_implementability_score: 1\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Assess MaaS 3.4 GA baseline changeability for xKS\n\n## Description\n\nDetermines which xKS-enabling changes can be made in the MaaS 3.4 GA codebase before downstream\nimplementation work commits to an approach. Three questions from the strategy's open questions and\nassumptions are resolved by one experiment (inspecting the 3.4 GA baseline and its release freeze\nconstraints):\n\n1. Can the existing MaaS AuthPolicy generation leverage Authorino's external OIDC identity source\n   without code changes, or are changes needed?\n2. Can the Tenant CRD be extended with a `groupClaimPath` field, or is it frozen in MaaS 3.4 GA?\n3. Can maas-api be instrumented with Prometheus metrics and structured logging within the\n   baseline, or must the instrumentation ship as a post-3.4 patch (or be deferred)?\n\nAlso confirms MaaS 3.4 RC/stable build availability with the MaaS PM (code freeze passed late\nMarch 2026; stable build delivery timing unknown — critical blocker for the Summit timeline).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Inspect the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline: Tenant CRD spec (existing `oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`,\n  `oidcAudience` fields; extensibility for a `groupClaimPath` field),\n  MaaSAuthPolicyReconciler's AuthPolicy generation, and maas-api instrumentation surface.\n- Determine release freeze constraints: whether CRD schema changes, controller changes, and\n  maas-api changes may land in the baseline or require a post-3.4 patch vehicle.\n- Confirm RC/stable build availability dates with the MaaS PM.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Each of the three questions has a documented answer with evidence from the baseline (file/CRD\n  references), not conjecture.\n- Explicit go/no-go on extending the Tenant CRD with `groupClaimPath`, selecting the conditional\n  implementation branch for group claim mapping (code change vs. documented claim format).\n- Recommended release vehicle (baseline change vs. post-3.4 patch vs. defer) for maas-api\n  observability instrumentation.\n- MaaS 3.4 build availability (RC and stable) confirmed with dates, or the timing risk escalated.\n\n==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E003.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E003\ntitle: Validate CKS observability infrastructure accessibility\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Monitoring\nteam: xKS Platform\ntype: Investigation\npriority: P0\ninvestigation_signals:\n  question_specificity: 1\n  source_accessibility: 0\n  local_runnability: 0\n  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n  human_judgment_required: -1\nimplementation_type: null\ndependencies: []\nai_signals: null\nai_implementability: Low\nai_implementability_score: -2\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Validate CKS observability infrastructure accessibility\n\n## Description\n\nValidates the strategy's assumption that CKS (CoreWeave Kubernetes Service) provides standard\nobservability infrastructure — a Prometheus instance able to scrape MaaS metrics endpoints and\nGrafana Loki log aggregation accessible for MaaS workload logs. This is infrastructure not in the\nplatform inventory, so its existence and accessibility must be confirmed before the observability\nintegration work commits to an approach; if inaccessible, dedicated observability provisioning\nbecomes part of the scope.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Confirm the CKS Prometheus instance can scrape MaaS `/metrics` endpoints (scrape target\n  reachability, required annotations or scrape configuration).\n- Confirm Loki ingests and makes queryable the logs of the namespaces where MaaS will deploy\n  (label filters usable for MaaS logs).\n- Document any access requirements, provisioning gaps, or configuration needed from CoreWeave /\n  the xKS Platform side.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Prometheus scrape of a representative MaaS-style `/metrics` endpoint on CKS is confirmed\n  working (or the gap and remediation documented).\n- Loki aggregation of workload logs from the MaaS target namespace is confirmed queryable with\n  label filters (or the gap and remediation documented).\n- Findings are documented, including any access coordination required with CoreWeave.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables [P0] Observability metrics functional (infrastructure validation portion).\n- Enables [P1] Observability logging functional (infrastructure validation portion).\n\n==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E004.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E004\ntitle: Helm chart packaging for MaaS components on xKS\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Model as a Service\nteam: MaaS\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 0\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: -1\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ndependencies: []\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 4\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\ngated_by: null\ngate_failure_impact: null\n---\n# Helm chart packaging for MaaS components on xKS\n\n## Description\n\nPackages the MaaS components (maas-api, maas-controller, payload-processing BBR ext_proc server)\nas Helm charts for non-OpenShift Kubernetes, replacing the OLM-based operator installation used\non RHOAI. The charts make every OpenShift-specific dependency configurable so the same charts\ndeploy on CKS (Istio), AKS, and other xKS platforms.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Helm charts for maas-api, maas-controller, and payload-processing with configurable values for:\n  - Gateway class name (Istio for CKS; `envoy-gateway` multi-platform and\n    `azure-application-gateway` for AKS as validated future options) instead of\n    `openshift-default`.\n  - OIDC issuer configuration (issuer URL, client ID, audience, group claim path).\n  - TLS certificate source via cert-manager instead of OpenShift service-CA auto-generated\n    certificates.\n  - PostgreSQL connection (shared with other RHAII components or dedicated — resolved at\n    deployment time, both supported).\n- Removal of OpenShift-specific assumptions from the deployment manifests.\n- Integration of the charts with the RHAII xKS deployment/installation strategy, in collaboration\n  with the xKS Platform team.\n- Build and publication of the charts through the RHAII build pipeline (Helm chart build pipeline\n  onboarding is a pending process gate per the strategy's prerequisites).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The chart installs maas-api, maas-controller, and payload-processing on a non-OpenShift\n  Kubernetes cluster with all listed values configurable.\n- TLS certificates are issued via cert-manager and rotation does not interrupt active requests.\n- The MaaS HTTPRoute attaches to a configurable GatewayClass (Istio validated on CKS).\n\n==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-E005.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-E005\ntitle: External OIDC authentication enablement in MaaS\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Model as a Service\nteam: MaaS\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: rewrite\n  fallback_approach: If the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline requires code changes to leverage\n    external OIDC (not just configuration), rewrite the epic around the required code\n    changes to the AuthPolicy generation and internal auth middleware; if the baseline\n    cannot be changed at all, ship the OIDC enablement as a post-3.4 patch.\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 1\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: -1\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 5\njira_key: null\nbranch: null\n---\n# External OIDC authentication enablement in MaaS\n\n## Description\n\nReplaces OpenShift-specific identity infrastructure (KubernetesTokenReview against the OpenShift\nAPI server) with external OIDC validation for MaaS on xKS. Customers provide their own\nOIDC-compatible identity provider (Keycloak, Entra ID, Google Identity Platform, Cognito, or\nother standards-compliant providers). The API key authentication flow (`sk-oai-*` bearer tokens\nvalidated via the maas-api callback endpoint) is OIDC-agnostic and remains unchanged — its\ncontinued function on xKS is validated here.\n\nThe approach is gated on the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline changeability assessment: the strategy expects\nbasic OIDC integration to be a configuration change (the Tenant CRD already supports\n`oidcIssuerURL`, `oidcClientID`, `oidcAudience`), but if the baseline requires code changes the\napproach is rewritten accordingly.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Configure the MaaS-generated AuthPolicy resources to use Authorino's `oidc` identity source\n  (customer OIDC issuer URL and JWKS endpoint) instead of KubernetesTokenReview.\n- Plumb OIDC issuer configuration through the Helm chart values and Tenant CRD fields.\n- Adapt internal maas-api authentication paths that rely on Kubernetes TokenReview /\n  SubjectAccessReview (OpenShift identity mapping) for the xKS deployment model.\n- Preserve the API key validation callback path (`/internal/v1/api-keys/validate` via Authorino\n  HTTP callback) and confirm it is unaffected by the OIDC change.\n- Validate against at least one standards-compliant OIDC provider, with the design supporting\n\n==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A-E006.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-A-E006\ntitle: Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping in MaaS\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Model as a Service\nteam: MaaS\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nbranch: A\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: remove\n  fallback_approach: 'If the Tenant CRD is frozen in MaaS 3.4 GA (outcome A does not\n    hold), drop this code-change epic and adopt the documentation approach: document\n    the required group claim format and push IdP configuration burden to customers.'\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 1\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 1\n  open_questions: 0\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: 0\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\nimplementation_type: null\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: High\nai_implementability_score: 5\njira_key: null\n---\n# Configurable OIDC group claim path mapping in MaaS\n\n## Description\n\nConditional branch (outcome A: the MaaS 3.4 GA baseline is extensible). Implements configurable\nOIDC group claim path mapping so the MaaSSubscription controller can map rate-limiting tiers to\nuser groups sourced from OIDC tokens with provider-varying claim formats (Entra ID `groups`,\nCognito `cognito:groups`, Google Identity Platform custom claims) instead of Kubernetes RBAC\ngroups.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add a `groupClaimPath` field to the Tenant CRD (default `auth.identity.groups`).\n- Pass the configured claim path through to the MaaSSubscription controller's CEL predicate\n  generation for tier/subscription mapping.\n- Expose the claim path as a Helm chart value for per-deployment configuration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given an OIDC deployment with a non-standard group claim format and `groupClaimPath` configured\n  to match, a user with group membership authenticating is mapped to the appropriate subscription\n  tier, measured by successful tier-based rate limiting enforcement.\n- The default claim path preserves current RHOAI behavior (backwards compatible, additive change).\n- Tier/subscription CRUD against OIDC group claims is functional.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n\n==> artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E006.md <==\n---\nepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8003-BRANCH-B-E006\ntitle: Document required OIDC group claim format for xKS identity providers\nparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8003\ncomponent: Documentation\nteam: MaaS\ntype: Implementation\npriority: P0\nimplementation_type: docs-authoring\nbranch: B\ngated_by: RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ndependencies:\n- RHAISTRAT-8003-E002\ngate_failure_impact:\n  action: remove\n  fallback_approach: If the Tenant CRD is extensible in MaaS 3.4 GA (outcome B does\n    not hold), drop this documentation epic and implement the configurable groupClaimPath\n    field in the Tenant CRD and subscription controller instead.\nai_signals:\n  change_specificity: 1\n  pattern_precedent: 0\n  adapter_pattern: 0\n  existing_foundation: 0\n  open_questions: 0\n  external_dependency: 0\n  human_process_gates: -1\n  repo_access: 1\n  architecture_claims: 1\ninvestigation_signals: null\nai_implementability: Medium\nai_implementability_score: 2\njira_key: null\n---\n# Document required OIDC group claim format for xKS identity providers\n\n## Description\n\nConditional branch (outcome B: the Tenant CRD is frozen in MaaS 3.4 GA and cannot be extended).\nDelivers the strategy's stated fallback for group claim mapping: document the required OIDC group\nclaim format and push identity-provider configuration burden to customers, so that customer IdPs\nemit groups at the claim path the MaaS subscription controller already reads\n(`auth.identity.groups`).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Author customer-facing documentation of the required group claim format for MaaS tier mapping\n  on xKS.\n- Per-provider configuration guidance: Entra ID (emitting `groups`), Cognito (mapping\n  `cognito:groups`), Google Identity Platform (custom claims), Keycloak (group claims).\n- Validation guidance for customers to verify their IdP emits the expected claim.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The documentation states the exact claim path and format MaaS expects for tier mapping.\n- Configuration guidance covers at least Entra ID, Cognito, and one standards-compliant provider\n  (e.g., Keycloak).\n- The documentation is technically reviewed against the MaaS subscription controller's actual\n  predicate behavior.\n- The guidance is validated with at least one real identity provider configuration.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a57964090eceb0b5","agent_id":"a4a6b6390e6cbdd43","session_id":"d2b1376c-e550-4ef4-93a1-44e607f6014e","uuid":"138b574d-0a88-477c-b400-c70e692a6563","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:12:02.022Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e4e943377640417","permission_decision":{"decision":"