ion.\n54\t- User identity flow documentation for xKS: OIDC issuer configuration, token validation,\n55\t  group-claim-based tier mapping, and API key lifecycle.\n56\t- Content split with the conditional group-claim deliverables: the per-provider claim-format\n57\t  configuration reference (exact claim path and format MaaS expects, per-IdP configuration\n58\t  guidance) is owned by the group-claim branch selected by the baseline changeability assessment\n59\t  — it is authored there, alongside the implementation or fallback it describes. This epic's\n60\t  identity-flow document covers the end-to-end flow (OIDC issuer configuration, token validation,\n61\t  API key lifecycle) and references that claim-format reference for the group-claim specifics\n62\t  rather than duplicating it. The direct dependency on the baseline changeability investigation\n63\t  ensures the selected outcome is known before authoring begins.\n64\t- Publication through the Red Hat docs content process.\n65\t\n66\t## Acceptance Criteria\n67\t\n68\t- A document exists listing all known limitations of MaaS on xKS versus RHOAI MaaS GA.\n69\t- The user identity flow for xKS deployments is documented (external OIDC, group claims, API\n70\t  keys, customer IdP responsibility).\n71\t- The documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented system (accuracy validated\n72\t  against what was built and validated on CKS/AKS).\n73\t\n74\t## HLR Traceability\n75\t\n76\t- [P1] Known limitations documented.\n77\t- [P2] User identity flow documented for xKS deployments.\n78\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba431fd5c6e583b5","agent_id":"a56ac38aeba4300c7","session_id":"d2b1376c-e550-4ef4-93a1-44e607f6014e","uuid":"e2b32095-13d5-4a14-9abf-d456b6ccde45","timestamp":"2026-10-22T09:20:08.396Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-be2ef31e31986cce","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRA